        "abstract": "<jats:p>We examine intraday lead-lag relationships in high-frequency stock returns that arise from firmspecific interactions. After removing systematic variation using common risk factors, we model lagged cross-dependencies in idiosyncratic returns with Kendall's correlation networks. Using S&amp;amp;P 500 constituent stocks, we document that lead-lag effects are both stronger and more persistent at higher sampling frequencies. In contrast to earlier evidence based on lower-frequency data, we find that these effects largely disappear in daily returns but remain pronounced at intraday horizons. A network-based trading strategy further shows that lead-lag signals extracted from high-frequency idiosyncratic returns have substantial predictive power and deliver significantly higher out-of-sample Sharpe ratios than signals based on low-frequency returns or returns unadjusted for systematic risk.</jats:p>",
        "URL": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6811380"
      }
